            "text": "Before the city wakes, there is a delicate hush that settles over the streets. The sky holds a promise, painted in shades of violet and gold, while the breath of dawn curls like mist around rooftops. In that hour, the familiar feels enchanted, as if the world is pausing to take a sip of silence. Birds test their voices, sending out tentative notes that float through the cool air. A lone jogger passes, the rhythm of footfalls soft against the pavement, and somewhere, a bakery door creaks open, releasing the scent of fresh bread. These small moments are easily swallowed by the noise and haste of the day, but when you catch them, they feel like gifts, reminders that time is made not only of obligations and tasks, but of countless breath pauses strung together. Standing at a window with a cup of coffee, watching the light change, you can sense your own edges softening. The day will come, with emails and errands and conversations, but for now there is only this gentle interval. It is a practice in being present, in noticing the subtle, in appreciating that something as ordinary as morning can feel extraordinary when we allow ourselves to linger. In that lingering, we make room for wonder, for gratitude, for the quiet idea that life is richer than the lists we write and the clocks we consult. The city stirs, but you carry a pocket of dawn, a softness threaded through the rest of your day, lingering."
